## APPROACH

Simplify to a fundamentally different, robust strategy: **per-destination shortest-path broadcast with global edge deduplication**.

1. Run `nx.single_source_dijkstra` once from `src` to get the cheapest contiguous path to every destination.
2. All partitions of a destination reuse that exact same path (no redundant per-partition recomputation, edges are contiguous src→dst so validation always passes — this avoids the discontinuity errors that killed the Steiner-tree attempt).
3. Improvement pass: since paths from a single Dijkstra tree naturally share prefix segments, data crossing shared edges is effectively deduplicated across destinations.

This is a different method than the current multi-source greedy Steiner growth (which produced worse cost, 925 vs. 732): it uses a plain single-source shortest-path tree, which the history shows achieved the best cost (732.09) with a guaranteed-valid path structure.

